From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter Wittenborn <hunter.wittenborn@fyder.ai>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:43:53 -0500
Subject: fix: wait on one Wayland configure at a time

WaylandWindow lets up to 3 applied configures wait for their frames at
the same time. During an interactive resize:

- The compositor sends a new size every few milliseconds, so several
  sizes are in flight at once.
- The renderer works on one resize at a time (RenderWidgetHostImpl's
  visual_properties_ack_pending_), so it skips sizes.
- viz unblocks the window frame for a skipped size as soon as a newer
  page frame activates (SurfaceAllocationGroup::OnFirstSurfaceActivation)
  and embeds the previous size's page frame in it
  (FindLatestActiveSurfaceInRange).

The window is then shown at one size with its page drawn at another. On
GNOME Wayland, dragging a window's left edge makes page-drawn buttons,
scrollbars and side panels at the right edge jitter.

GTK only has one size in flight at a time, the newest:

- It acks each configure on arrival and keeps only the latest size
  [1][2].
- It draws that size, then freezes until the compositor's frame
  callback [3][4].

Limiting WaylandWindow to 1 does the same here: it applies the newest
configure only once the previous one's frame is done, skipping the
sizes in between. The limit of 3 was added for Lacros [5], which
doesn't seem to give a reason for the value.

Measured on Ubuntu 26.04.1 (GNOME 50, Wayland) by counting recorded
frames with misplaced page-drawn buttons during fast left-edge drags.
Both setups used the default resize deadline, which Electron sets on
Wayland. The counts fluctuate a lot between drags, so each setup ran
three times:

- 3 in flight: 2, 1 and 6 bad frames (9 of 822)
- 1 in flight: 0, 0 and 0 bad frames (0 of 802)

This patch should be upstreamed. It can be removed once Chromium waits
on one configure at a time.

[1]: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gtk/-/blob/4.22.0/gdk/wayland/gdktoplevel-wayland.c#L680
[2]: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gtk/-/blob/4.22.0/gdk/wayland/gdktoplevel-wayland.c#L705
[3]: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gtk/-/blob/4.22.0/gdk/wayland/gdksurface-wayland.c#L457-476
[4]: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gtk/-/blob/4.22.0/gdk/wayland/gdksurface-wayland.c#L267-286
[5]: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/4074455/21/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window.cc#1184

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window.cc b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window.cc
index 55347cf96ebcf517e8e719ab9dd71689dde6f86f..444fe5e1f9f3bf2221b3db208d590f1de5c06ab2 100644
--- a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window.cc
+++ b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/wayland_window.cc
@@ -1655,8 +1655,8 @@ void WaylandWindow::MaybeApplyLatestStateRequest(bool force) {
         in_flight_requests_,
         [](const StateRequest& req) { return req.applied; });
 
-    // Allow at most 3 configure requests to be waited on at a time.
-    constexpr int MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS = 3;
+    // Allow at most 1 configure request to be waited on at a time.
+    constexpr int MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS = 1;
     if (in_flight_applied >= MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS) {
       applying_state_ = false;
       return;
